---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated={updated} schema={schema}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">A renter with years of on-time payments can still have a thin credit file, because rent usually isn't reported. Most landlord rent-collection apps now sell an add-on that reports it. They differ on which bureaus get the data, whether past months can be added, what it costs and whether the landlord can pay. In California, some landlords must offer it.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>The short version.</strong> <strong>RentRedi</strong> reports to all three bureaus for $3.99–$5.99 a month <Src href={S.rr} label="RentRedi" />. <strong>Innago</strong> reports to TransUnion free, or to all three for $5.99 a month <Src href={S.inn} label="Innago" />. <strong>TurboTenant</strong> reports to TransUnion and Experian for $4.99 a month <Src href={S.tt} label="TurboTenant" />. <strong>Avail</strong> reports to TransUnion at $3.95 per reported month <Src href={S.av} label="Avail" />. All four describe reporting on-time (positive) payments; none of them reports late rent through these products.</div>

