  <h2>California: larger landlords must offer it</h2>
  <p>Since January 1, 2025, California requires landlords to offer each tenant on the lease the option of having positive rental payment information reported to at least one nationwide consumer reporting agency <Src href={S.ca} label="Civ. Code 1954.07(a)" />. Key details:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Timing:</strong> for leases signed on or after April 1, 2025, at signing and at least once a year after; for leases outstanding on January 1, 2025, by April 1, 2025 and yearly after <Src href={S.ca} label="(b)" />.</li>
    <li><strong>The written offer</strong> must say reporting is optional, name each bureau, state any fee, explain how to opt in and out, and note that a tenant who opts out can't resume for six months <Src href={S.ca} label="(d)" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Fee:</strong> the lesser of the landlord's actual cost or $10 a month, and nothing if the landlord has no cost. Nonpayment can't be reported, can't be grounds for eviction and can't be taken from the deposit <Src href={S.ca} label="(g), (h)" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Exemptions:</strong> buildings with 15 or fewer units, unless the landlord owns more than one rental building and is a REIT, a corporation, or an LLC with at least one corporate member; and assisted housing developments <Src href={S.ca} label="(j)" />.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Does reported rent help an applicant you're screening?</h2>
  <p>Rent reported this way becomes part of the applicant's credit file at the bureau that receives it; RentRedi says TransUnion recommends 12 months of on-time payments "for the greatest potential impact" <Src href={S.rr} />. Which bureau your screening service pulls from matters: the services on our <a href="/tenant-screening-report-comparison/">report comparison</a> list which bureau supplies their credit report.</p>

